ity.\ndf['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'] = np.log1p(df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS'])\ndf['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] = np.log1p(df['SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'])\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column RADIUS_RATIO is dropped\n# RADIUS_RATIO shows low variance for identifying unique relationships. It is less likely to add value due to the lack of extreme distinguishable patterns it offers, as evidenced by its moderately narrow IQR and relatively consistent mean and std.\ndf.drop(columns=['RADIUS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has a range dominated by values close to zero (mean = 6.3866, std = 4.8045) and does not exhibit significant patterns likely to differentiate vehicle types. Its contribution to classification appears limited.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS, while somewhat informative, has low variance and high concentration of values within a small range. Other variables, like KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MAJOR_AXIS, capture broader variance and can serve similar purposes.\ndf.drop(columns=['KURTOSIS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end", "```python\n# Step 1: Add the square of the RADIUS_RATIO\n# Feature description: Square of RADIUS_RATIO to emphasize larger discrepancies between max and min radii, which might better capture the unique contours of each silhouette class.\n# Usefulness: RADIUS_RATIO already measures the variability in radii. Squaring this metric magnifies larger variations, which may help differentiate vehicle types like BUS (potentially more uniform) vs. SAAB (potentially more irregular).\n\ndf['RADIUS_RATIO_SQUARE'] = df['RADIUS_RATIO'] ** 2\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Compute the ratio of SCATTER_RATIO to ELONGATEDNESS\n# Feature description: Ratio of SCATTER_RATIO (inertia about minor/major axis) to ELONGATEDNESS (area/shrink width squared).\n# Usefulness: This ratio reflects the spatial distribution of mass relative to elongation. For example, a VAN could have higher elongation but more evenly distributed mass compared to a BUS, aiding in the vehicle type classification.\n\ndf['SCATTER_ELONG_RATIO'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] / (df['ELONGATEDNESS'] + 1e-8)  # Add small constant to avoid division by zero\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Apply z-score normalization to CIRCULARITY\n# Feature description: Z-score normalization of the CIRCULARITY feature.\n# Usefulness: Standardizing CIRCULARITY ensures it contributes proportionately to the model, preventing domination by other features with higher variances or scales. This is particularly important since CIRCULARITY has a relatively smaller range.\n\ndf['CIRCULARITY_ZSCORE'] = (df['CIRCULARITY'] - df['CIRCULARITY'].mean()) / df['CIRCULARITY'].std()\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping columns that may add noise or redundancy\n# Explanation: MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO has a very narrow range ([2, 55]) compared to other features and might not provide much new information for classification.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping columns that may add noise or redundancy\n# Explanation: SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS has low variability (mean = 6.39, std = 4.8), indicating that it may not sufficiently differentiate vehicle types.\ndf.drop(columns=['SKEWNESS_ABOUT_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
