2026-05-24 01:40:52,562 - INFO - thoughts: ["Here are three possible strategies for feature engineering to improve predictions for classifying vehicle silhouettes. Each step is backed by reasoning to leverage useful attributes of the dataset and to enhance the downstream model's performance.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Create ratio-based composite features for shape consistency\nCertain features like `COMPACTNESS`, `CIRCULARITY`, and `DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY` describe geometric properties of the silhouette. Combining these in a ratio format can help highlight shape consistency or variations.\n\n```python\n# CIRCULARITY_TO_COMPACTNESS_RATIO\n# Combines `CIRCULARITY` and `COMPACTNESS` to encode relative proportions of circularity and compactness, which can help differentiate vehicle types based on curvature and area efficiency.\n# Usefulness: Vehicles with similar compactness may have different appearances based on curvature (e.g., bus vs. van). Ratios amplify these complementary signals.\ndf['CIRCULARITY_TO_COMPACTNESS_RATIO'] = df['CIRCULARITY'] / df['COMPACTNESS']\n\n# DISTANCE_TO_CIRCULARITY_RATIO\n# Combines `DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY` and `CIRCULARITY` to highlight differences in spread and edge-feature proximity.\n# Usefulness: Some vehicles (e.g., BUS) have larger frame spreads, making this feature particularly effective.\ndf['DISTANCE_TO_CIRCULARITY_RATIO'] = df['DISTANCE_CIRCULARITY'] / df['CIRCULARITY']\n\n# CIRCULARITY_SCALED_RADIUS_PRODUCT\n# Multiplies `CIRCULARITY` by `SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION` to encode the interaction between curvature and spatial distribution.\n# Usefulness: This feature emphasizes silhouettes' rotational symmetry and their spatial spread. Intuitively, buses and vans, for example, would have distinct spreads compared to cars like OPEL or SAAB.\ndf['CIRCULARITY_SCALED_RADIUS_PRODUCT'] = df['CIRCULARITY'] * df['SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Generate logarithmic or transformed/scaled features\nAttributes that span large numerical ranges (e.g., `ELONGATION_EFFECT`, `SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS`) may require logarithmic transformations. This reduces skewness and enhances the model's ability to generalize.\n\n```python\n# LOG_ELONGATION_EFFECT\n# Log transformation of `ELONGATION_EFFECT` to handle its wide range without losing differentiation.\n# Usefulness: Reduces numerical scale and makes the feature distribution more Gaussian-like, improving model convergence and interpretability.\ndf['LOG_ELONGATION_EFFECT'] = np.log(df['ELONGATION_EFFECT'] + 1)\n\n# LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_DIFF\n# Difference between log-transformed `LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS` and `LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS`.\n# Usefulness: Helps capture the disparity between spatial variances in minor and major axes after scaling, which can delineate variations in shape along the axes.\ndf['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_DIFF'] = df['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'] - df['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MAJOR_AXIS']\n\n# ROOT_SCATTER_RATIO\n# Square root transformation of `SCATTER_RATIO`.\n# Usefulness: Moderately transforms a high-variance feature to provide meaningful variation across classes while controlling for extreme values.\ndf['ROOT_SCATTER_RATIO'] = np.sqrt(df['SCATTER_RATIO'])\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Feature interactions & group-level insights for silhouette shape granularity\nCombining features like elongation, rectangularity, and scatter provides more complex understanding of vehicle shapes.\n\n```python\n# ELONGATION_RECTANGULARITY_RATIO\n# Ratio of `ELONGATEDNESS` to `PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY`.\n# Usefulness: Allows comparison of a silhouette's thinness relative to its rectangularity. For instance, buses (often rectangular) will have lower ratios compared to rounded cars or vans.\ndf['ELONGATION_RECTANGULARITY_RATIO'] = df['ELONGATEDNESS'] / df['PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY']\n\n# SCATTER_TO_VARIANCE_RATIO\n# Ratio of `SCATTER_RATIO` to `SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION`.\n# Usefulness: Encodes the spread (scatter) of the silhouette relative to its spatial distribution or gyration scale. Highlights variations in asymmetry across object classes.\ndf['SCATTER_TO_VARIANCE_RATIO'] = df['SCATTER_RATIO'] / df['SCALED_RADIUS_OF_GYRATION']\n\n# GROUPBY_COMPACTNESS_MEAN_SHIFT\n# Calculates deviation of each entry's `COMPACTNESS` from the mean `COMPACTNESS` of its class (derived via group mean).\n# Usefulness: Adds contextual information to account for intra-class compactness variation. Buses, for instance, generally have smaller compactness deviations compared to smaller cars.\ncompactness_mean = df.groupby(df['Answer'])['COMPACTNESS'].transform('mean')\ndf['GROUPBY_COMPACTNESS_MEAN_SHIFT'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] - compactness_mean\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping redundant or correlated features\nAfter creating new features, some existing features with high correlation or low explanatory power might need to be removed.\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# This feature ('MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY') may be highly correlated with other rectangularity-based dimensions (e.g., `PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY`), which are already enhanced through engineered features.\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# This feature ('HOLLOWS_RATIO') adds little variance, as it focuses on hollow regions in silhouettes that are unlikely to distinguish between similar vehicle shapes in this task.\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\nThese steps collectively aim to enhance feature quality, reduce redundancy, and improve the downstream model's ability to classify silhouettes accurately.", "```python\n# Step 1: Create interaction features by multiplying certain columns\n# Feature name: INTERACTION_COMPACTNESS_PR_AXIS\n# Feature description: This feature is created by multiplying COMPACTNESS and PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This can help capture the interaction effect between the compactness of the silhouette and the aspect ratio of the principal axis, which may provide more discriminative power for identifying different vehicle types.\n\ndf['INTERACTION_COMPACTNESS_PR_AXIS'] = df['COMPACTNESS'] * df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Apply logarithmic transformation to high-value skewed features\n# Feature name: LOG_ELONGATION_EFFECT\n# Feature description: This feature is created by applying the log transformation on ELONGATION_EFFECT to reduce its skewness.\n# Usefulness: The ELONGATION_EFFECT feature has a large range of values (1316-5934) and is likely to be skewed. Log transformation can normalize it, making it more suitable for machine learning models.\n\ndf['LOG_ELONGATION_EFFECT'] = df['ELONGATION_EFFECT'].apply(np.log)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Ratio of MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO to PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO\n# Feature name: RATIO_MAX_PR_AXIS_ASPECT\n# Feature description: This feature is created by dividing MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO by PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO.\n# Usefulness: This captures the relationship between two geometric ratios associated with the shape. Variations in this relationship may differentiate vehicle types based on their silhouette proportions.\n\ndf['RATIO_MAX_PR_AXIS_ASPECT'] = df['MAX.LENGTH_ASPECT_RATIO'] / df['PR.AXIS_ASPECT_RATIO']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column HOLLOWS_RATIO is dropped\n# HOLLOWS_RATIO appears to have a very tight range (181-211), and its distribution with a small standard deviation (7.5056) implies limited variation among examples.\n# It's less likely to contribute meaningful distinctions among the different vehicle classes. In addition, it might add noise to the model.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['HOLLOWS_RATIO'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS is dropped\n# LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS and SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS appear to be highly correlated because one is the log transformation of the other.\n# To avoid redundancy and multicollinearity, we drop the logarithmic version, assuming the original scaled version may harbor more granularity.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['LOG_SCALED_VARIANCE_ALONG_MINOR_AXIS'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY is dropped\n# MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY overlaps semantically with several other rectangularity measures in the dataset, such as PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY.\n# Removing it reduces potential redundancy. Furthermore, its statistical range (~118-186) overlaps largely with PR.AXIS_RECTANGULARITY, suggesting it might not add unique information.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['MAX.LENGTH_RECTANGULARITY'], inplace=True)\n```end"]
